 - **[Screen Model] vt100 0.16.2 as a pre-send readiness gate only.** After `turn-ended`, the gate waits for the screen to go quiet, then requires the absence of every known modal signature and waits, up to its maximum wait, for the version-stamped input-box signature. As landed, the model is the pure `viola_agent_claude::screen::Screen` (no I/O, no clock read; every instant is a parameter), and `run` feeds it on its own feed thread (`src/run/gate.rs`): a tee on the pump output writes the human's bytes first and then offers a copy with `try_send` into a queue bounded at `FEED_CAPACITY` = 256 messages (`sync_channel`; the pump copies at most 8192 bytes per write, so at most 2 MiB waits for the model), so the passthrough never waits on the model. The `Screen` sits behind a mutex shared by the feed thread and the `Gate` handle `send` reads (`Gate::wait_ready`, re-read every 20 ms); each feed and resize runs under `catch_unwind` inside that lock. A caught vt100 panic poisons the model until the host size changes, and so does a message the full queue drops (a dropped byte desynchronises the screen): one `parse-rejected{parser:"vt100-feed", detail:"oversize"}` per poisoning episode, and a size message carries the drop count at its offer, so a drop after a queued resize poisons the fresh screen again. A poisoned model reads `input-not-ready`. The quiet period (`QUIET_PERIOD` = 300 ms) and the maximum wait (`GATE_MAX_WAIT` = 8.5 s) are compiled built-ins in `viola-agent-claude`, validated per CLI version by the stamped `quiet-period` row (the ready and turn screens settle within `GATE_MAX_WAIT`). The maximum wait was 5 s until chunk 2026-10-07-send-waits-out-the-paste-hint raised it on the founder's live ruling of 2026-10-07T10:29Z (relayed by the overseer), so that it covers the CLI's 8.0 s paste hint ([CLI Version Compatibility]); the quiet period is unchanged. As measured on 2.1.287 at that chunk (`evidence/reverify-round.md`), the ready screen settled in 1 103 ms and the turn screen in 617 ms. The signatures are compiled in `viola_agent_claude::screen::SIGNATURES`: `input_box: ["for agents"]` and `modals: ["Yes, I trust this folder", "Yes, allow external imports"]` (`Signatures::holds_any`). A screen is ready when a row contains an input-box literal and no row contains a modal literal. The per-version `modal-signature` row (an untrusted start shows a compiled modal literal) and `input-box-signature` row (the ready and turn screens hold the input-box literal and no modal) validate them. They landed with chunk 2026-10-05-real-cli-verify-probes, through the typed-input `viola verify` probe the founder ratified live on 2026-10-05 (relayed by the overseer), which runs the installed `claude` locally on the dev host only. `run` passes `Some(&SIGNATURES)` to `gate::start` exactly when its version gate read `cli_verified` (`Launched.cli_verified`): the full gate on a verified CLI version, and the partial gate otherwise. The verdict's consumer is confirmed `send`. On a verified CLI a modal row refuses at once, and a quiet screen with no input-box row keeps the gate waiting for one until `GATE_MAX_WAIT` has passed since the send entered the gate. A screen not quiet within the maximum wait, a poisoned model and a quiet screen still without an input-box row at the bound are refused: `send` types nothing and reports `not-delivered` with detail `input-not-ready`. The wait lives in the pure verdict (`Screen::verdict` reads `waiting_since`), so `Gate::wait_ready` did not change for it. Rationale: CLI-native modals bypass every hook (measured on 2.1.280: the "Teach auto mode…" modal swallowed a paste), and the screen lags the hooks. Signatures are compiled literals that each version's stamp validates. On a CLI version with no passing stamp the gate is partial: `Screen::verdict(None, …)` still refuses a poisoned model and a screen not quiet within the maximum wait, and a quiet screen is ready with no row read, so delivery confirmation decides the rest (the overseer's F1 reading of "falls back to delivery confirmation only", 2026-10-04). `Screen::rows()` is the only row-text exit, and only `viola verify` reads it. As measured at chunk 2026-10-05-real-cli-verify-probes (research M13 and M19, the dev host's 2.1.288), the CLI's trust is inherited from a trusted parent folder. The external CLAUDE.md imports approval is keyed on the git root instead, so a subfolder of a repo whose import is unanswered shows "Allow external CLAUDE.md file imports?" until that answer is set. viola types into neither dialog.
-- **[Delivery Confirmation] Every `send` is confirmed.** If no matching `prompt-submitted` arrives within the confirmation window, the send is reported as `not-delivered` with detail `no-prompt-submitted`. The confirmation window is the compiled `CONFIRM_WINDOW_FALLBACK` = 10 s in `viola_agent_claude::screen`, never in `config.json`. It is no longer provisional: the per-version `confirm-window` ledger row validates it (the typed prompt reaches UserPromptSubmit within the window, measured as `measured.typed_probe.prompt_latency_ms`), and `run` reads no window number from the stamp. `send` blocks for at most the readiness gate's maximum wait, plus the atomic paste, plus the window (18.5 s with the compiled 8.5 s and 10 s), and returns its result no later than when the window closes. On the MCP surface that total must stay below the MCP client's tool-call timeout. Matching compares the typed text (the sent text with every CR LF pair and every other CR as one LF, and without its trailing CR and LF characters, below) with `prompt-submitted`'s `text` exactly (nothing else is trimmed), whatever `origin` the hook filed, `harness` included, after `viola-agent-claude` has normalised it: first the `<pasted_content id=…>` wrapper the CLI puts around long pastes is removed together with the frame the CLI writes around it (the two newlines directly before the open tag, the one directly after the close tag, and a second after it when text follows that is not the next pair's own frame), then the CLI's escaping of tag-like text is reversed (the landed `long-paste-wrapper` and `tag-escaping` rows). The wrapper-side matcher therefore never sees a Claude-specific shape, and a literal `<\pasted_content` the user typed is never mistaken for the wrapper. `send` types a text with every CR LF pair and every other CR inside it as one LF, and without its trailing CR and LF characters (trailing LF: the founder's ruling, live, 2026-10-07T15:21Z, relayed by the overseer, landed at chunk 2026-10-07-a-send-ending-in-a-newline-is-confirmed; trailing CR and CRLF: the founder's ruling, live, 2026-10-09, relayed by the overseer, landed at chunk 2026-10-09-epoch-3-cleanup; a CR or a CR LF inside a text, typed as the LF the CLI submits: the founder's ruling, live in the overseer's dialog, 2026-10-09T16:51Z, the options shown, relayed by the operator, landed at chunk 2026-10-09-inner-cr-and-crlf-in-a-sent-text). `validate_paste_text` runs first, on the text as received. Directly after it the typed text is taken once (`viola_agent_claude::hook::typed_text`: every CR LF pair as one LF, every other CR as one LF, and every trailing CR and LF removed, in any order; it holds no CR, and nothing else changes, not a TAB, a space, or an LF that is not at the very end), and from there it is the only text `send` uses: the local-command classification, the in-flight text the match compares, `text_bytes` and the paste. A send whose text ends in one or more CR or LF characters (LF, CR, CRLF, or several of them) is therefore confirmed like any other: its `prompt-submitted` is relabelled `driver`, it ends `ok` `{submitted_at, cursor}`, and no `wheel` record is appended. The reason is a CLI behaviour (as measured on 2.1.287 on a verified and an unverified home at chunk 2026-10-07-live-rows-and-paste-shapes-on-the-dev-host, `evidence/hint-window.md` step 7, `live-shape-red-green.md`): the CLI drops a pasted text's last newline before UserPromptSubmit, wrapped or not, so a text typed with it came back one byte short and the exact match failed. That measurement stands, and no `send` relies on it now. A text whose typed text is empty (the empty text, or a text of only CR and LF characters) is refused `not-delivered` / `empty-text` (exit 13) and is never issued: by the client before any frame (`refusal_of` in `src/cmd/send.rs`, the second product caller of `typed_text`, beside the wrapper's `send`) and by the wrapper directly after the typed text is taken and before the first wheel read, with nothing reserved, issued or typed, no cursor, and the wheel and the running-turn state not read (the founder's ruling, live, 2026-10-09, relayed by the overseer; landed at chunk 2026-10-09-epoch-3-cleanup). This is proved under the fake agent, at the unit and cross-process layers on the three CI OSes. As measured on live `claude` 2.1.287 on the Linux dev host at chunk 2026-10-08-first-live-test-and-self-drive (`evidence/live-readings.ndjson`): a send ending in two newlines is typed without them (`text_bytes` 31 of 33 bytes sent), its prompt is filed `driver`, it is confirmed, and no `wheel` record follows. On that build, before chunk 2026-10-09-epoch-3-cleanup, a text of only newlines was issued with `text_bytes` 0, drew no `prompt-submitted`, and ended `not-delivered` / `no-prompt-submitted` (exit 13) when the window closed, with the wheel unmoved; and a text ending in one CR was typed with the CR (`text_bytes` 32), the CLI submitted it without the CR, the exact match failed, the prompt was filed `human`, the wheel moved to the human and the send ended `not-delivered` / `no-prompt-submitted` (exit 13). Both stand as records of the earlier build. As measured on live `claude` 2.1.287 on the Linux dev host at chunk 2026-10-09-epoch-3-cleanup (`evidence/live-run.md`, `evidence/live-readings.ndjson`): before the change, texts ending in CR LF and in CR CR were each submitted by the CLI without the whole ending, filed `human` and not confirmed (`trailing-crlf`, `trailing-cr-cr`); after it, texts ending in CR, CR LF and CR CR are each typed without the ending, filed `driver` and confirmed with no `wheel` record (`after-cr`, `after-crlf`, `after-cr-cr`), and a text of one CR LF is refused by the client, exit 13, `not-delivered` / `empty-text`, with no record in the wrapper (`after-empty-text`). Before chunk 2026-10-09-inner-cr-and-crlf-in-a-sent-text a CR or a CR LF inside a text was typed as it stood: the CLI submitted either as one LF, the typed text still held the CR, the exact match failed, the prompt was filed `human`, the wheel went to the human, the turn ended, and the send ended `not-delivered` / `no-prompt-submitted` (exit 13), so a delivered and answered driver text was reported not delivered and took the wheel from the driver (`after-inner-crlf`, `after-inner-cr`, as measured on 2.1.287 at chunk 2026-10-09-epoch-3-cleanup; both stand as records of the build before the rule). With the rule the typed text carries the LF itself, so a send whose text holds a CR or a CR LF inside is one paste of the typed text and is confirmed like any other, and no `send` types a CR or relies on what the CLI does with one. It is pinned under the fake agent at the unit tier and across processes, by reading the typed bytes. As measured on live `claude` 2.1.287 on the Linux dev host at chunk 2026-10-09-inner-cr-and-crlf-in-a-sent-text (`evidence/live-run.md`, `evidence/live-readings.ndjson`; one headless session of six sends, a local live measurement): texts holding one inner CR LF, one lone inner CR, two CRs in a row, an LF then a CR, and CR LF after two of three lines were each filed `driver` and confirmed with no `wheel` record and no CR in the submitted prompt (`rule-inner-crlf`, `rule-inner-cr`, `rule-inner-cr-cr`, `rule-inner-lf-cr`, `rule-crlf-lines`). The same session read first, as its control, that an LF typed inside a bracketed paste is submitted unchanged (`rule-inner-lf`). Every multi-line `send` relies on that shape, and it has no ledger row and no `viola verify` probe: its row is owed to `v1-34` and is the working-route entry "Paste newline ledger row" (placed at the head of Epoch 5 on the founder's word, live in the overseer's dialog, 2026-10-09T20:58Z, relayed by the operator). Not measured: any CLI version but 2.1.287, a long or wrapped multi-line text (every text was under 100 bytes and of at most three lines), an LF typed inside a paste on Windows, and what the CLI does with a typed CR CR or LF CR (no `send` types one). As landed (the `send` module: `SendSlot::claim` / `settle` and `append_hook_event` in `src/run/send/slot.rs`), the wrapper claims the in-flight send's matching prompt, appends it with `origin:"driver"`, and only then settles the send with that line's `ts` as `submitted_at`, so `prompt-submitted` always precedes `send-confirmed` in `events.ndjson`. Skills are expanded into a prompt and fire UserPromptSubmit like any prompt (measured: `/andromeda-arch`), so a slash send that is not on the ledger's local-command list is an ordinary send, and if it is not confirmed the result is `not-delivered`. A built-in local command fires no UserPromptSubmit, so the ledger lists the known local commands, each with its post-condition or "none". If a listed command's measured post-condition is met, the send returns `ok`, confirmed. For `/clear`, that post-condition is a SessionStart with source `clear` and a new `session_id`. If a listed command's post-condition is "none" or not yet measured on this CLI version (`/remote-control` today), the send returns `ok` with `{"confirmed":false,"detail":"unconfirmable"}`. Nothing else is ever unconfirmable. Any other unconfirmed send is `not-delivered`. The decision depends on the ledger, never on a leading slash. The local-command list and the `/clear` row are compiled (`viola_agent_claude::ledger::LOCAL_COMMANDS`: `/clear` with the post-condition "a new session", `/remote-control` with none; row `local-command-clear`, stamped on 2.1.287 by chunk 2026-10-06-local-command-and-paste-framing-rows through the ratified typed-input probe), and `send` consumes them (as landed by chunk 2026-10-06-local-command-send-outcomes; the decision is `classify` in `src/run/send/classify.rs`). When the send's slot is reserved the typed text is classified by exact equality with a list entry: nothing else trimmed, no case folding, never a leading-slash test. A listed command followed by newlines is therefore that command: `/clear` and a newline is `/clear`, and since chunk 2026-10-09-epoch-3-cleanup so is `/clear` and a CR or a CRLF (the LF consequence widened by the CR strip, pinned at the unit layer) (the founder was shown the LF consequence with the 2026-10-07 ruling above; proved at the unit layer, and as measured live on 2.1.287 at chunk 2026-10-08-first-live-test-and-self-drive, `evidence/live-readings.ndjson`, `clear-then-newline`: `/clear` and one LF, `text_bytes` 6, confirmed by a new session of cause `clear`, no `prompt-submitted`, no `wheel` record). Every refusal rung applies to a listed command as to any text. A listed command whose post-condition is "none", and any listed command on a CLI version that is not verified (`/remote-control` on every version, `/clear` with no passing stamp), is typed and answered `unconfirmable` at once: `send-issued`, the paste, then the record `send-confirmed {cursor, confirmed:false}` and the `ok`, with no confirmation window. The verified bit is the version gate's `cli_verified`, handed to `SendSlot::new`; the send path reads no `ledger/stamps.json`. `/clear` on a verified version waits the window for its post-condition only, and no `prompt-submitted` claims it. The hook tap claims it on a `session-start` whose `cause` is `clear` and whose `agent_session_id` is a string that differs from the id the send slot remembers, appends that line unchanged, and only then settles the send with that line's `ts` as `submitted_at`, so the `session-start` precedes `send-confirmed`. The remembered id is the `agent_session_id` of the last `session-start` the tap took, whatever its cause, kept in memory under the in-flight send's lock: a line with no string id clears it, with nothing remembered any string id is new, and nothing persists it. If the window closes first the send is `not-delivered` / `no-prompt-submitted` with its cursor (no new detail). The opening sentences of this entry, and the prompt claim, describe a send that is not a listed command. The `/clear` confirmation is proven on the recorded 2.1.287 `clear-1` variants replayed by the fake agent; against a live CLI it is as measured on 2.1.287 on the Linux dev host at chunk 2026-10-08-first-live-test-and-self-drive (`evidence/live-run.ndjson`, its `clear` line): a driver `/clear` was confirmed by a `session-start` with cause `clear` and a new `agent_session_id`, with no `prompt-submitted`, and the next skill was sent into the cleared session. Rationale: a turn boundary per the hooks does not prove the input box is ready. Confirmation is the backstop behind the screen gate. Local commands would otherwise be false negatives (measured: `/remote-control` fired no UserPromptSubmit), and `/clear` matters most because drivers clear between pipeline skills.
